WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.510
GRANT: Hey cloud developers.

00:00:01.510 --> 00:00:02.460
My name is Grant.

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:03.703
VINNY: And my name is Vinny.

00:00:03.703 --> 00:00:05.370
GRANT: And we're here
to get you started

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.050
using Google Cloud Functions.

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:10.050
Google Cloud Functions makes
it easy to run and scale

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:13.782
your code in the cloud that
listens and responds to events.

00:00:13.782 --> 00:00:15.240
VINNY: The Functions
Framework lets

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.920
you write lightweight functions
that run in many environments,

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.280
including Cloud Functions,
your local development

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.070
machine, Cloud Run
and Cloud Run on GKE,

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:25.110
and Knative-based environments.

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:26.922
The library is open
source on GitHub.

00:00:26.922 --> 00:00:29.130
And the Functions Framework
is used in the production

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.350
by Google Cloud Functions.

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:33.720
So Grant, what is the
Functions Framework?

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.600
GRANT: The Functions
Framework defines an interface

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.550
for building modular services.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:42.270
This means that
rather than emulating

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:44.890
how your function will
run in production,

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:48.390
you can see exactly how your
code will perform when testing

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:49.980
it on your local machine.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.870
The Functions Framework
is also Knativ-compatible,

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:54.840
supporting Knative
eventing, which

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.260
makes it possible to port
your functions between Knative

00:00:58.260 --> 00:00:59.400
environments.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.260
Specifically, the
Functions Framework

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:05.790
defines a port for
specifying HTTP functions,

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:10.080
a target name for the function,
and then lastly, a signature

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.678
type, either an HTTP or
event, for the type of event.

00:01:13.678 --> 00:01:15.970
VINNY: The Functions Framework
can be used in two ways,

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:18.360
either as a CLI, a Command
Line Script, that you

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.090
run in your terminal to
start the function server,

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:24.570
useful for local testing, or
as a library, a dependency

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.110
for your application.

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:28.320
This is useful for staging
a production Google Cloud

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.580
Functions environment
on your machine.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:32.370
You can specify an
explicit dependency

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:34.362
on a version of the
Functions framework.

00:01:34.362 --> 00:01:35.820
And if you don't
specify a version,

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.550
Google Cloud Functions will
automatically choose the latest

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:39.840
Functions Framework.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.930
Now let's look at that
open-source framework for Node.

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:46.230
It's used in production for
Google Cloud Functions running

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:48.120
Node 10.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.912
Within the package, the
module uses Express.

00:01:51.912 --> 00:01:56.220
Express is a fast,
unopinionated, minimalistic web

00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:01.270
framework for Node.js that makes
developing services a breeze.

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:04.140
It's a project of the
Node.js Foundation,

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:06.588
and it's used by the
Functions framework.

00:02:06.588 --> 00:02:08.380
GRANT: Let's get started
with the Functions

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:11.050
framework for Node.js.

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:15.728
Let's first run the command npm
install @google cloud/functions

00:02:15.728 --> 00:02:16.538
framework.

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:23.950
This will create a package.json
in your current directory.

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:26.710
Create an index.js file
with the following contents.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:32.110
So what are we doing here?

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:36.230
Well, we're exporting a
function called Hello, World.

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:39.240
The function accepts
two parameters, req,

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:42.810
for the express request
object, and res,

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:45.630
for the express response object.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:50.720
Next, we're sending the string,
Hello, World, in the response.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:53.350
To start the server, run
the following command--

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:58.160
npx @google
cloud/functions framework.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:02.060
Open localhost in your
browser, and see Hello, World.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:03.332
Awesome.

00:03:03.332 --> 00:03:05.540
VINNY: So you your function
running on your computer,

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:09.020
but how do you get it running in
production, like in a real URL?

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:10.460
GRANT: Good point, Vinny.

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:13.190
Since the Functions Framework
is the same library that

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:16.760
runs Google Cloud Functions,
we can deploy our same Node

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:20.100
application with
no modifications.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:23.360
So let's deploy the code
to Google Cloud Functions.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:26.390
Using gcloud in our
directory with our function,

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:29.300
we can call gcloud
functions deploy,

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.070
specifying our function name--

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:34.520
trigger with HTTP--
and using Node 10.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:37.550
After a bit, we'll
get a live URL.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:40.700
If I copy the URL and paste
it into my web browser,

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:42.800
I'll invoke my function.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.450
But where did our
console.log statements go?

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:46.430
Vinny?

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:49.130
VINNY: Well, on my machine, I'll
see the logs in the terminal

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:50.670
where I ran the function.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.970
But in the cloud, I'll
see them on Stackdriver.

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:54.560
This is because
Google Cloud Functions

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.840
has an integration
with Stackdriver where

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:59.150
logs written to standard
out or standard error

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:02.060
will appear directly
in Cloud Console.

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:04.400
GRANT: And that's like Google
Cloud Functions Framework.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.233
To learn more about the
Functions Framework,

00:04:06.233 --> 00:04:07.460
check it out on GitHub.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:08.960
We have so many
useful guides on how

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:12.920
to do things like how to deploy
the Cloud Run using TypeScript,

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:15.010
using the Node
Debugger, et cetera.

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:18.467
The link to the GitHub repo
is in the description below.

00:04:18.467 --> 00:04:21.050
VINNY: And be sure to subscribe
right here on the Google Cloud

00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:23.840
Platform channel on YouTube
to get notified of new videos

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:27.700
about Cloud Functions we'll
see you here next time.

